<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Freemasonry</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Free-Masonry</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Freemasonry consists of fraternal groups that trace their origins to the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> of stonemasons and is considered the oldest existing secular fraternal organisation, with documents and traditions dating back to the 14th century. Its basic unit is the lodge, supervised regionally by independent Grand Lodges, of which the United Grand Lodge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, dating from June 1717, is the oldest. Members pass through three degrees, Entered Apprentice, Fellow of the craft and Master Mason, taught through symbols and allegory. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> and some Protestant denominations have opposed it, and Freemasons have been persecuted by authoritarian states.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Since the middle of the 19th century Masonic historians have looked for the movement's origins in the Old Charges, a series of similar documents running from the Regius Poem, the oldest known Masonic text, written in Middle English and originating in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a> around 1425, to the beginning of the 18th century. Addressed to operative masons' lodges, they relate the craft to a mythologised history, set out the duties of its grades and say how oaths of fidelity are taken on joining. The 15th century also saw the first evidence of ceremonial regalia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 18th century Freemasons formed an international network that often met in secret and promoted the ideals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. English Freemasonry spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the 1720s, first as lodges of expatriates and exiled Jacobites, and from France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to most of Continental Europe. By 1789 there were between 50,000 and 100,000 French Masons. The Grand Orient de France was reconstituted in 1773, and in 1877 it removed the requirement to believe in God, after which many French Masons founded the Grande Loge Nationale Française in 1913. The earliest known American lodges were in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, where John Moore wrote of attending one in 1715.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> lodges were increasingly suppressed in the 18th century and were reintroduced by exiles returning after 1905. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-kerensky">Alexander Kerensky</a> was a Mason, and the organisation collapsed when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> took power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> outlawed Freemasonry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the mid-1920s, and the Nazis suppressed it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1935, when many Masons in Germany and occupied countries were executed or sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The lodge is the basic unit, supervised by an independent Grand Lodge or Grand Orient, and no body governs all of Freemasonry; links between jurisdictions rest on mutual recognition. The United Grand Lodge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, dating from June 1717, is regarded as the Mother Grand Lodge. The symbolism is drawn mainly from stonemasons' tools, such as the square and compasses, the level and plumb rule and the trowel. James Anderson's first printed constitutions of the 1720s excluded women, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> has forbidden Catholics to join since Pope Clement XII's bull of 1738.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Freemasonry” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alexander I gave the viceroy, Grand Duke Konstantin Pavlovich, almost dictatorial powers, abolished press freedom in 1819, ordered <b>Freemasonry</b> abolished in 1821, and from 1825 the Sejm met in secret, while Nikolay Novosiltsev's secret police pursued Polish societies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Haitian Vodou developed among enslaved Africans in Hispaniola, taking form in Saint-Domingue around the mid-18th century from Fon, Kongo and Yoruba religions joined with Catholicism and <b>Freemasonry</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It marks the beginning of organised modern <b>Freemasonry</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-24">24 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
